WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:09.710
So I'm here to talk about
model migration from Excel to R.

00:00:09.710 --> 00:00:16.560
But I'd like to actually begin
this presentation with a story.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:21.000
So this is a story about
a model migration exercise

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.770
that I did about five
or six years ago.

00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:28.700
And it was from Excel to R,
subject of this talk. And

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:30.350
the ultimate outcome
of this story

00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:33.890
was that I really improved
my programming skills

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:34.730
in the language R.

00:00:34.730 --> 00:00:38.720
So, you know, this is a
positive story, right?

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:40.940
But I don't think it would
be interesting to tell

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:44.470
a story about just good
things happening to me.

00:00:44.470 --> 00:00:47.030
I think the best
stories have conflict

00:00:47.030 --> 00:00:50.750
and in the best stories,
things go wrong.

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:52.850
And this is a story,
I'm pleased to say,

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:55.340
where the price of my
success is that I ended up

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:57.690
crashing the server.

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:03.390
And it's doubly tragic because
not only did this happen to me,

00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:04.900
I was also having to use Excel

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:05.673
day in, day out.

00:01:05.673 --> 00:01:07.090
And I know people
in the audience,

00:01:07.090 --> 00:01:08.632
if you've had that
experience before,

00:01:08.632 --> 00:01:10.470
that can be a bit of a struggle.

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:14.640
So let me take you back
five or six years ago.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:16.000
Where did this all start?

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:19.740
So this started with
my boss at the time.

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:22.300
This is my boss.

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:25.770
And my boss comes over
to me and he says,

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:29.580
Johnny, I've got a job for you.

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:31.810
And the job is this.

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:37.100
So we have a model that we've
configured in Microsoft Excel.

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:39.740
It's slightly complicated.

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:42.770
It takes multiple
people, multiple days

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:45.680
to actually execute
the model, and it's

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:48.200
highly error prone process.

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:49.790
So at the end of
it, we often have

00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:51.950
to check that if
things have gone wrong,

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:55.310
there's a lot of validation
involved in this process.

00:01:55.310 --> 00:01:58.640
And personally, I think would be
better suited to a programming

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:01.860
language like R. So would you
be interested in doing this?

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:04.980
So my instinctive reaction
is, yeah, that sounds great.

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:06.200
What's the model?

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:11.180
So my boss goes on to explain
it's a statistical model, OK?

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:14.340
And it's also a
simulation model.

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:18.270
So at the time I was working
in financial services.

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:20.640
And this was for
a banking client.

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:25.340
And what they were interested
in was a multiple simulations

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:27.920
of yearly loss scenarios.

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:31.400
And after those simulations
had been executed,

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:32.990
what they wanted
to see was, well,

00:02:32.990 --> 00:02:35.420
if you plotted them on a
distribution, what would

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:38.780
the 99.9% percentile look like?

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:42.380
And if you think about that,
for a yearly loss distribution,

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:45.750
that's an event that would only
happen once in 1,000 years.

00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:47.310
So it's pretty rare.

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:51.800
And the point about this is this
was not only a complicated model,

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:54.090
it was something that
was quite intensive.

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:55.850
It needed to be
run multiple times,

00:02:55.850 --> 00:02:58.950
and I'm talking 10 to
15 million iterations.

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:03.320
So, you know, I was aware of
the challenge that lay before me

00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:07.940
and so I dived into the Excel
model and it's, you know,

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:10.337
it's kind of hard to explain
to people who haven't worked

00:03:10.337 --> 00:03:12.170
in financial service
or industries like this

00:03:12.170 --> 00:03:14.990
before, where you come
across these Excel models

00:03:14.990 --> 00:03:18.960
and they're monolithic, they're
trying to do everything.

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:20.810
And I think this
picture kind of sums

00:03:20.810 --> 00:03:26.360
up the feeling of walking
into one of these things.

00:03:26.360 --> 00:03:28.400
It's a bit like
booking an Airbnb

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:30.650
and you turn up in
a demolition house.

00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:33.080
But I was resilient.

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:35.330
I was a resilient individual
and I understood the task

00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:35.940
before me.

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:38.850
And I wanted to carry this out
and prove that I could do this.

00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:44.370
So I opened up a
script, a single R script,

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:48.840
and I proceeded to copy the
contents of the Excel model

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:51.020
into the script.

00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:53.210
And I basically
did this verbatim

00:03:53.210 --> 00:03:56.090
because quite honestly,
I didn't fully

00:03:56.090 --> 00:03:59.550
understand what was going
on in the Excel model.

00:03:59.550 --> 00:04:01.650
And I'd also written
on my CV that I

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:05.440
was an advanced user of R,
which wasn't quite, quite true.

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:08.910
But, you know, we'll
live with that.

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:10.930
And I proceeded to write
the whole thing up.

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:12.970
This was painful,
but I got through it.

00:04:12.970 --> 00:04:15.900
I was really proud of
myself, and I kind of

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:18.690
thought that by the end of this,
like, my boss is really going

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:20.339
to be impressed with this.

00:04:20.339 --> 00:04:22.950
It's going to really
change the business.

00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:26.130
So I go away to
lunch, I click Run.

00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:28.210
And I'm like, great happy days.

00:04:28.210 --> 00:04:30.150
I'm on cloud nine.

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:35.090
Then I come back from lunch and
there's a message in my inbox.

00:04:35.090 --> 00:04:40.470
And somebody has sent an email
and it's not addressed to me.

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:42.110
It's addressed to the team.

00:04:42.110 --> 00:04:44.330
That's the first kind
of sign that you've

00:04:44.330 --> 00:04:45.710
done something really wrong.

00:04:45.710 --> 00:04:48.440
And someone says words
to the effect of,

00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:51.140
I can't log on to the server.

00:04:51.140 --> 00:04:52.880
Now I'm a junior
analyst at the time,

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:56.720
so I don't really understand
the consequences that I

00:04:56.720 --> 00:04:58.560
can create for other people.

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:01.370
So my reaction to this
is, well, that's a bit

00:05:01.370 --> 00:05:05.390
of a shame for you, but I hope
you solve the problem soon.

00:05:05.390 --> 00:05:08.970
And then inevitably, you
start getting other messages.

00:05:08.970 --> 00:05:11.720
So someone else says,
yeah, I can't log on.

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:14.930
Someone else inevitably needs
this server for critical work

00:05:14.930 --> 00:05:17.023
and you're blocking it.

00:05:17.023 --> 00:05:18.690
And then eventually
someone comes to you

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:21.450
and they're like, do you
know what's going on, Johnny?

00:05:21.450 --> 00:05:25.000
And my initial reaction is, no.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:27.210
But then it quickly dawns
on me that I kind of

00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:30.750
do have some idea of
what's going wrong.

00:05:30.750 --> 00:05:34.330
That very last shred of
denial is quickly eroding.

00:05:34.330 --> 00:05:36.780
And I finally realized
that something I've done

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:39.210
has caused this
mess, and I realized

00:05:39.210 --> 00:05:41.130
that I crashed the server.

00:05:41.130 --> 00:05:44.400
Now, I put a
celebratory emoji there

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:46.830
because I think this is a
bit of a badge of honor.

00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:49.920
If you're ever involved in
this, you know, treat it

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:50.770
as a badge of honor.

00:05:50.770 --> 00:05:52.920
Don't treat it as
a total failure.

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:55.770
You will come through
better in the end.

00:05:55.770 --> 00:05:59.820
So this was, I jest  about this.

00:05:59.820 --> 00:06:02.450
And it's kind of a funny
experience in hindsight,

00:06:02.450 --> 00:06:05.840
but at the time this was
kind of a serious issue.

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:09.580
So I was working
an industry that is

00:06:09.580 --> 00:06:11.660
dominated by Microsoft Excel.

00:06:11.660 --> 00:06:14.860
In fact, Excel is
like a fortress.

00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:16.870
Most models are
configured in Excel,

00:06:16.870 --> 00:06:19.960
and Excel is the most
trustworthy piece

00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:24.580
of software for many senior
executives in these scenarios.

00:06:24.580 --> 00:06:27.530
There's a quote from The Wire.

00:06:27.530 --> 00:06:29.500
I don't know if
anybody's seen The Wire.

00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:31.570
The character Omar
says, if you're

00:06:31.570 --> 00:06:34.690
going to come at the
King, you best not miss.

00:06:34.690 --> 00:06:39.830
And I just come at the King
and I'd missed pretty badly.

00:06:39.830 --> 00:06:42.790
I was lucky enough to have
a boss at the time who

00:06:42.790 --> 00:06:47.650
was sympathetic enough to
persevere with this project

00:06:47.650 --> 00:06:52.180
beyond my drastic failure.

00:06:52.180 --> 00:06:56.510
And so what's the
moral of this story?

00:06:56.510 --> 00:06:58.620
Uh, why am I here?

00:06:58.620 --> 00:07:00.760
What kind of advice
can I give you?

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:03.080
Let's analyze what
went wrong here.

00:07:03.080 --> 00:07:08.600
So the first big tip
I have and it's really

00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:11.420
the biggest fundamental
problem that I encountered here

00:07:11.420 --> 00:07:15.570
is you don't really want
to copy or replicate Excel.

00:07:15.570 --> 00:07:18.410
OK? Now, like, this
is an obvious point

00:07:18.410 --> 00:07:20.750
to make to people if you're
a seasoned programmer,

00:07:20.750 --> 00:07:24.620
but let's take a step
back and put yourselves

00:07:24.620 --> 00:07:26.780
in the shoes of somebody
who hasn't really written

00:07:26.780 --> 00:07:28.760
much code in their life.

00:07:28.760 --> 00:07:31.350
So somebody who's only
ever used Excel before.

00:07:31.350 --> 00:07:33.710
For somebody like
that, everything

00:07:33.710 --> 00:07:38.940
looks a bit like this
and that's a spreadsheet.

00:07:38.940 --> 00:07:41.270
But if you think
about it visually,

00:07:41.270 --> 00:07:46.410
it's a bit like a data frame
in R, and that's not a mistake.

00:07:46.410 --> 00:07:48.090
Excel models

00:07:48.090 --> 00:07:50.090
a lot of things
using data frames

00:07:50.090 --> 00:07:53.150
and whilst this isn't
a problem in itself,

00:07:53.150 --> 00:07:54.920
you end up with a
lot of Excel models

00:07:54.920 --> 00:07:58.710
which have a few
questionable design choices.

00:07:58.710 --> 00:08:02.510
So the first issue you
encounter typically in the wild

00:08:02.510 --> 00:08:07.460
is Excel models, which start
out with a few number of tabs

00:08:07.460 --> 00:08:10.620
of slowly increasing
levels of information.

00:08:10.620 --> 00:08:12.800
And you think we're
OK, we're stable,

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:15.330
and then by the end of it
you've got hundreds of tabs.

00:08:15.330 --> 00:08:18.260
And if you're the
creator of this model,

00:08:18.260 --> 00:08:20.450
you typically end up having
a nervous sweat any time

00:08:20.450 --> 00:08:21.840
anybody else needs to use it.

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:24.770
It's a very precarious
situation to be in.

00:08:24.770 --> 00:08:29.180
And all of this comes about
because the most natural data

00:08:29.180 --> 00:08:31.130
structure to use when
you're using Excel

00:08:31.130 --> 00:08:34.580
is a data frame object.

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:38.130
So this is one issue that
you encounter sometimes.

00:08:38.130 --> 00:08:41.960
The second is a lot
of these models,

00:08:41.960 --> 00:08:45.570
you can store data just in a
spreadsheet or in a data frame.

00:08:45.570 --> 00:08:48.810
So you don't really
need a database, right?

00:08:48.810 --> 00:08:50.370
Well, not quite.

00:08:50.370 --> 00:08:52.310
I mean, a lot of
these models probably

00:08:52.310 --> 00:08:55.490
should be supported by a
database, but they're not.

00:08:55.490 --> 00:08:58.820
And that's nobody's fault.
Right? Like a lot of analysts

00:08:58.820 --> 00:09:01.430
who have built these
models don't necessarily

00:09:01.430 --> 00:09:06.710
have the knowledge or the skills
to even think about databases,

00:09:06.710 --> 00:09:09.050
let alone implement a database.

00:09:09.050 --> 00:09:12.020
Certainly at the time
when I was doing this,

00:09:12.020 --> 00:09:15.710
I wasn't aware of
this as an option.

00:09:15.710 --> 00:09:17.640
Now, why am I
mentioning all this?

00:09:17.640 --> 00:09:19.250
These are issues
with Excel models,

00:09:19.250 --> 00:09:24.080
but the point is, if you're
migrating an existing

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:26.480
model into an R
environment, you definitely

00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:30.200
don't want to be copying
over these design choices.

00:09:30.200 --> 00:09:32.060
If you do this, then
you're only going

00:09:32.060 --> 00:09:36.690
to reintroduce the problems
in a different context.

00:09:36.690 --> 00:09:40.180
So what's my next tip
off the back of this?

00:09:40.180 --> 00:09:43.860
So what you want to do
instead of copying what's

00:09:43.860 --> 00:09:47.100
going on in the existing
platform, which is Excel

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:50.700
in this case, you want to
think about the structure

00:09:50.700 --> 00:09:51.550
of this model.

00:09:51.550 --> 00:09:54.090
So what you want
to do is abstract

00:09:54.090 --> 00:09:57.125
away the implementation
that you see in Excel

00:09:57.125 --> 00:09:58.500
and think about,
well, what is it

00:09:58.500 --> 00:10:00.390
actually trying to do, right?

00:10:00.390 --> 00:10:02.500
What are the functional
requirements?

00:10:02.500 --> 00:10:04.690
What is this model
trying to achieve?

00:10:04.690 --> 00:10:08.100
And the first fundamental
step of this process

00:10:08.100 --> 00:10:11.560
is where does my model
begin and where does it end?

00:10:11.560 --> 00:10:13.920
And that can be pretty
tricky with some

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:15.400
of these Excel models.

00:10:15.400 --> 00:10:18.510
They are so monolithic,
that it's actually

00:10:18.510 --> 00:10:21.060
very hard sometimes to know
where the calculations begin

00:10:21.060 --> 00:10:22.560
and where they end.

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:24.030
But this is a
process that you have

00:10:24.030 --> 00:10:26.880
to go through in order to
start unpicking this object

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:29.300
and migrating it across.

00:10:29.300 --> 00:10:31.800
So this is why I have this
A to B presented here.

00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:36.130
So you want to how do I get
from point A to point B?

00:10:36.130 --> 00:10:38.110
That's the first ingredient.

00:10:38.110 --> 00:10:42.140
The second thing is
well along that route.

00:10:42.140 --> 00:10:46.660
What are the operations that I'm
applying to the data at point A

00:10:46.660 --> 00:10:51.070
to get to point B? And if you're
doing a migration, especially

00:10:51.070 --> 00:10:54.100
in R or any
language, really, these

00:10:54.100 --> 00:10:57.220
are really important things
to identify because you'll

00:10:57.220 --> 00:10:59.950
be able to encapsulate these
into functions and objects

00:10:59.950 --> 00:11:02.890
that you can use in your
programming environment.

00:11:02.890 --> 00:11:06.160
So this is a good kind
of planning scheme

00:11:06.160 --> 00:11:09.340
to go through before you
actually do anything.

00:11:09.340 --> 00:11:11.770
And the second thing
kind of related to this

00:11:11.770 --> 00:11:15.100
is you want to think about what
are the data structures

00:11:15.100 --> 00:11:19.390
that I need to transport
the data along this route.

00:11:19.390 --> 00:11:21.910
OK just because the Excel
model that you're looking at

00:11:21.910 --> 00:11:24.460
is doing everything
in Excel and you've

00:11:24.460 --> 00:11:27.430
got multiple tabs and multiple
bits of information hanging

00:11:27.430 --> 00:11:29.500
in memory, it
doesn't mean that you

00:11:29.500 --> 00:11:33.310
need to do the exact same thing
when you migrate this into R.

00:11:33.310 --> 00:11:35.680
In fact, you could
probably save a lot

00:11:35.680 --> 00:11:39.730
of the hassle, a lot of
the overuse of this memory

00:11:39.730 --> 00:11:43.060
and all the other problems that
come with it by just thinking

00:11:43.060 --> 00:11:47.410
about what do I need to
retain as I go from A to B?

00:11:47.410 --> 00:11:51.940
So let me kind of put this
into context with an example.

00:11:51.940 --> 00:11:55.660
So I mentioned earlier that I've
been working on a simulation

00:11:55.660 --> 00:11:57.340
model at this time.

00:11:57.340 --> 00:11:59.290
Now, not to go into
too much detail,

00:11:59.290 --> 00:12:02.020
but the simulation
model in each outcome,

00:12:02.020 --> 00:12:03.770
it would generate
one simulation,

00:12:03.770 --> 00:12:08.320
which had a set of 30
new numeric outputs

00:12:08.320 --> 00:12:11.370
across 30 different
risk classes.

00:12:11.370 --> 00:12:16.590
And one stage of
this model is involved

00:12:16.590 --> 00:12:20.340
the task of trying to aggregate
these simulations together.

00:12:20.340 --> 00:12:23.400
So the author of the Excel
model decided that, well,

00:12:23.400 --> 00:12:26.805
the best idea that we
can use in this context

00:12:26.805 --> 00:12:28.680
is we'll take the current
simulation that you

00:12:28.680 --> 00:12:30.560
can see on the screen here.

00:12:30.560 --> 00:12:33.260
And then we'll take the
previous simulations.

00:12:33.260 --> 00:12:35.360
OK? the previous
set of simulations

00:12:35.360 --> 00:12:38.480
will add that on to the
existing set and then

00:12:38.480 --> 00:12:40.160
we'll get another
set of simulations

00:12:40.160 --> 00:12:43.610
that is one row bigger
than the previous.

00:12:43.610 --> 00:12:47.090
And that would go on recursively
until you got to, in this case,

00:12:47.090 --> 00:12:49.640
the 15,000,000th iteration.

00:12:49.640 --> 00:12:52.010
So, yeah, this was
something you needed

00:12:52.010 --> 00:12:53.810
to execute across
multiple spreadsheets

00:12:53.810 --> 00:12:55.800
and it was a slow process.

00:12:55.800 --> 00:12:58.560
Um, but here's the
problem, right?

00:12:58.560 --> 00:13:00.890
If you kind of
replicate this approach

00:13:00.890 --> 00:13:03.320
and you just stick
it in an R script,

00:13:03.320 --> 00:13:06.620
you're going to run into
trouble really quickly

00:13:06.620 --> 00:13:09.210
and it'll look something
a bit like this.

00:13:09.210 --> 00:13:12.590
And this is like
a classic problem

00:13:12.590 --> 00:13:16.100
that you'll encounter in the
R programming world, which

00:13:16.100 --> 00:13:20.460
is copying objects in
loops unnecessarily.

00:13:20.460 --> 00:13:25.250
And unfortunately, like this
is one of the key design flaws

00:13:25.250 --> 00:13:29.870
that I had first implemented
in my script, which

00:13:29.870 --> 00:13:32.870
meant that this thing would
just eat up your memory

00:13:32.870 --> 00:13:34.672
and it would keep
on going for ages.

00:13:34.672 --> 00:13:36.380
You, you probably
wouldn't get to the end

00:13:36.380 --> 00:13:39.440
of this on 15 million
iterations, at least

00:13:39.440 --> 00:13:40.920
within a few days.

00:13:40.920 --> 00:13:43.910
So this was a really
like key design

00:13:43.910 --> 00:13:46.520
flaw in the way I'd
structured this.

00:13:46.520 --> 00:13:49.290
So how did I fix
this in the end?

00:13:49.290 --> 00:13:53.210
So I thought about it
in a more abstract way,

00:13:53.210 --> 00:13:56.720
and I abstracted
away from whatever

00:13:56.720 --> 00:13:58.460
the model was doing initially.

00:13:58.460 --> 00:13:59.960
And I thought, well,
what I actually

00:13:59.960 --> 00:14:05.660
need to generate here is a
list of simulation results,

00:14:05.660 --> 00:14:07.790
and that will be equal to
the length of the number

00:14:07.790 --> 00:14:10.360
of simulations I want to run.

00:14:10.360 --> 00:14:14.740
And for each of these
entries in this list,

00:14:14.740 --> 00:14:17.890
I just need a function that will
generate my simulation results

00:14:17.890 --> 00:14:20.470
across these 30 risk classes.

00:14:20.470 --> 00:14:24.850
And then I just need to
run this again, and again

00:14:24.850 --> 00:14:27.790
until I get to the very end.

00:14:27.790 --> 00:14:31.570
And then we just need
to bind these together.

00:14:31.570 --> 00:14:36.190
So this looks simple and it was
kind of a subtly different way

00:14:36.190 --> 00:14:40.150
of designing this, but this
very different way of doing this

00:14:40.150 --> 00:14:43.720
was actually like a
massive improvement

00:14:43.720 --> 00:14:47.300
on the existing script
that I'd written.

00:14:47.300 --> 00:14:49.805
And it's small tweaks
like that where

00:14:49.805 --> 00:14:51.680
you think about the
requirements, what you're

00:14:51.680 --> 00:14:54.380
trying to do, which have
quite a significant effect

00:14:54.380 --> 00:14:56.070
on the performance.

00:14:56.070 --> 00:15:00.090
So my key tip here
as I've said already,

00:15:00.090 --> 00:15:02.810
is to structure your model.

00:15:02.810 --> 00:15:06.720
And the final tip I have here
is try to keep things simple.

00:15:06.720 --> 00:15:09.613
So one of the problems
is and I don't

00:15:09.613 --> 00:15:11.530
know what possessed me
to do this at the time,

00:15:11.530 --> 00:15:14.650
but as a budding
analyst, I thought

00:15:14.650 --> 00:15:16.540
it might be a good
idea to try and do

00:15:16.540 --> 00:15:19.790
a bit of parallel
processing in this script.

00:15:19.790 --> 00:15:22.300
And I think this
was the straw that

00:15:22.300 --> 00:15:25.150
broke the camel's
back in my case,

00:15:25.150 --> 00:15:27.220
coupled with some of
the other design flaws

00:15:27.220 --> 00:15:28.300
that I'd implemented.

00:15:28.300 --> 00:15:31.780
I was trying to do
multi-threaded processing

00:15:31.780 --> 00:15:34.120
and it all went a bit wrong.

00:15:34.120 --> 00:15:36.250
I didn't really read the
documentation properly

00:15:36.250 --> 00:15:40.760
and I kind of was
overcomplicating things, right?

00:15:40.760 --> 00:15:44.340
Like, was this really a concern
that I needed to address?

00:15:44.340 --> 00:15:45.410
The answer is no.

00:15:45.410 --> 00:15:49.810
Like what I really needed
to do with this process was

00:15:49.810 --> 00:15:52.900
start simple and start
with a sample of iterations

00:15:52.900 --> 00:15:55.570
that I'm trying to run, and
then I want to build this up,

00:15:55.570 --> 00:15:59.930
gradually, profile what I'm
doing and work from there.

00:15:59.930 --> 00:16:02.290
So doing the basics
right in these cases

00:16:02.290 --> 00:16:05.020
can be really important.

00:16:05.020 --> 00:16:08.220
So with that.

00:16:08.220 --> 00:16:11.180
I want to thank
you for listening,

00:16:11.180 --> 00:16:13.760
and I want to just leave
you with this quote

00:16:13.760 --> 00:16:18.815
from Bob Ross, which I really
like, is that you make mistakes

00:16:18.815 --> 00:16:20.190
and we all make
mistakes in life.

00:16:20.190 --> 00:16:22.910
And it's a cliche, but
I like the addition

00:16:22.910 --> 00:16:25.520
that mistakes are just
happy accidents, right?

00:16:25.520 --> 00:16:28.400
Like now I can jest
about this and I

00:16:28.400 --> 00:16:30.590
can be happy about my
accident because I actually

00:16:30.590 --> 00:16:33.500
learned a lot in the long run.

00:16:33.500 --> 00:16:38.230
So thank you all
for listening and.

